        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What a bilingual answering service actually does</h2>
        <p>
          An answering setup from us does four things on every call that
          reaches it, at any hour, in English or in Spanish. The call gets
          finished, not just taken down.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Answers in your business's voice.</strong> The caller hears your business name and your greeting, not a call center's. You approve the English greeting and the Spanish greeting. The setup does not invent a greeting you did not sign off on.</li>
          <li><strong>Qualifies the caller by your rules.</strong> You decide what every caller is asked, which calls book, which calls dispatch, and which calls go to a person you name. A Spanish call uses the questions you approved for that call. It does not add a question you did not write.</li>
          <li><strong>Books onto your real calendar.</strong> A qualified caller gets a real slot on the calendar you already use, not a message you retype in the morning. The language of the call does not change the slot. The setup does not invent an open slot.</li>
          <li><strong>Follows up by SMS.</strong> Confirmations and next-step texts go out after the call, inside the rules you approved. Every call is recorded and transcribed so you can review what was said.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The difference from a traditional answering service is the third
          step. A traditional service takes a message and hands the work back
          to you. This setup finishes the booking during the call, when your
          rules say to book. It is not a translation desk, and it is not a
          promise that every call type has been written in Spanish until you
          have heard that call type.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Why English-only call handling leaks revenue</h2>
        <p>
          A caller who cannot finish the call does not book. If the only
          greeting, the only questions, and the only confirmation are in
          English, a caller who needs Spanish hangs up or calls the next
          business that can complete the conversation. That is the leak. It
          is a missed booking, not a marketing problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are not publishing a revenue figure for that leak. We do not have
          one we are willing to print, and a made-up ticket total would not
          change the missed call. We are also not publishing a count of
          callers who prefer Spanish. If your own callers prefer Spanish, you
          already know that. This page will not invent the number for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          English-only handling fails in two places. The live call never
          qualifies the caller, so nothing lands on the calendar. Or the first
          ring was already missed, and the follow-up is still English-only.
          The follow-up path, separate from this page, is
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back for NWA businesses</a>.
          A text is not a substitute for answering when the line can be
          answered. Answering is not a substitute for the text when you
          already missed it. The language of that text is a script decision.
          We demonstrate it on the assessment. We do not promise it here.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What the setup handles in Spanish, and what it does not</h2>
        <p>
          What it handles is the script you approved. Greeting, qualification
          questions, booking language, a note that the caller was speaking
          Spanish, and escalation to a person you name. You hear those lines
          before they take a live customer call. Nothing goes live until you
          sign off.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it does not do is improvise. It does not invent a translation
          of a diagnosis, a legal intake, a price, or a policy you did not
          write. It does not add a clinical protocol. It does not tell a
          caller someone is on the way when your list says they are not. It
          does not claim a certification. If a call leaves the rules, it
          escalates to the person you named, or it takes the message you
          defined. It does not guess.
        </p>
        <p>
          What our build can and cannot do in Spanish, today, is something we
          demonstrate on the assessment call rather than promise in print.
          Bring a real call type. You will hear it. We will not claim a
          language result we have not played for you.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Springdale and Rogers: where the Spanish-preferring caller base is most visible</h2>
        <p>
          The visible evidence is hiring, not a census of callers. Employers
          in Springdale and Rogers advertise for Spanish-speaking front-desk
          staff. The settings in those ads are dental, medical, and food
          processing. That is a hiring fact, not a count of your callers, and
          we are not publishing a count.
        </p>
        <p>
          We did not count the ads. We are not publishing a share of the
          population, a percentage, or a language-population figure. A hiring
          ad means an employer in that city asked for Spanish at the front
          desk. It does not mean we measured your line. If your callers prefer
          Spanish, the setup has to handle that or it is not answering for
          the calls those ads exist to cover. If they do not, say so on the
          assessment. We will not sell you a Spanish script you do not need.
        </p>
        <p>
          This section is the statewide note on where that hiring fact shows
          up. It is not a Springdale city page and not a Rogers city page.
          Shift schedules, plant counts, and event nights belong on those city
          pages. They are not facts this page is willing to reprint as
          language proof.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Bilingual call handling by trade</h2>
        <p>
          The phone layer is the same. The rules are not. You write the
          questions for the desk you actually run. We do not publish a
          trade-by-trade language count, and we will not invent one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Dental and medical front desks.</strong> The hiring ads in
          Springdale and Rogers include these settings. A scheduling call
          while staff are with a patient is the same coverage problem in
          either language. The setup asks the questions you approved and books
          the slot. It does not add a clinical protocol you did not write, and
          it does not claim a certification.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Food-processing and plant offices.</strong> The same hiring
          fact includes food-processing settings in Springdale and Rogers. A
          vendor call or a front-desk call still has to be finished. We are
          not publishing employee counts, plant counts, or shift hours. Those
          hours are yours. You write what a call in that window should do.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Contractors.</strong> Emergency versus booking is a rule you
          write, not a language setting. The trade page for that dispatch is
          <a class="text-link" href="/after-hours-answering-service-contractors-nwa/">after-hours answering for NWA contractors</a>.
          If you want the page to say the caller was speaking Spanish, that is
          a question in the script, and the person you named for those calls
          is the one who gets it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Property managers.</strong> Tenant calls do not respect
          office hours. Their side of the handoff, separate from language, is
          on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/property-management-answering-service-northwest-arkansas/">property management answering page</a>.
          We are not publishing a tenant-language figure. You still only
          dispatch to the vendor list you already agreed to.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">After-hours and emergency dispatch in two languages</h2>
        <p>
          The same rules run at 2 p.m. and at 2 a.m. Nights and weekends are
          not a second product and not a second rate. A caller who needs
          Spanish after the desk goes home is the same leak as a caller who
          needs Spanish at noon, with nobody left in the office to catch it.
        </p>
        <p>
          You write what counts as an emergency before the first after-hours
          call. When a call matches a rule you approved, the setup reaches the
          person you named with the caller's name, callback number, address,
          and the problem the questions captured. If you wrote the language
          question, the handoff notes that the caller was speaking Spanish.
          The person who receives it is the person you named. We do not keep a
          roster of our own, and we do not send someone you did not name.
        </p>
        <p>
          A call that does not match an emergency rule books the next slot or
          takes the message you defined. It does not get a guessed emergency
          because the caller was speaking Spanish. Language is not an
          emergency line. The contractor page has the trade rules. This page
          does not borrow a threshold it did not state.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Booking onto your real calendar, in either language</h2>
        <p>
          A qualified caller gets a real slot on the calendar you already use,
          whether the call was in English or in Spanish. The setup does not
          invent an open slot, and it does not ask you to switch tools to get
          the booking. If a field the calendar needs is not in the script, it
          does not get invented.
        </p>
        <p>
          The phone layer is this service. The calendar layer, separate from
          the phone, is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">booking software</a>
          setup. Language does not create a second calendar. If you want the
          notes to record that the caller was speaking Spanish, that is a line
          you approve. The slot is still the slot your calendar had open.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a job needs a look before a price, it books the look. The setup
          quotes only a range you put in the script, or nothing. It does not
          invent a ticket total in either language.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How setup works when your callers speak two languages</h2>
        <p>
          The timeline is the same as any other Arkansas office. The extra
          decision is which call types you want heard in Spanish, who should
          receive those calls, and which lines the script may not translate
          because you have not written them.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Free 15-minute assessment.</strong> We load your business details and you hear the setup answer as your company, including a call type you want in Spanish. Nothing is due at that call.</li>
          <li><strong>Script and rules build.</strong> We write the English greeting, the Spanish greeting, the qualification questions, the emergency definitions, and the escalation paths with you. Nothing takes a real customer call until you sign off.</li>
          <li><strong>Launch.</strong> Live on your number after you sign off. Every call recorded and transcribed. The calendar connection is the one you already use.</li>
          <li><strong>Monthly tuning.</strong> We review real calls with you once a month and adjust the rules, including the Spanish lines, as the office changes.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          We are not publishing a specific day count. We did not measure one
          for this page. The work is the greeting, the questions, the calendar
          you already use, and the person you named for a Spanish call. It is
          not a custom software project.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Pricing for bilingual coverage</h2>
        <p>
          One published price sheet statewide, the same everywhere in Arkansas.
          Bilingual coverage is not a priced add-on and not a different rate.
          We are not estimating a language adder.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Setup:</strong> $2,500</li>
          <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> $497 flat, managed service</li>
          <li><strong>Included:</strong> 24/7 answering in your business's voice, English and Spanish call handling inside the script you approve, caller qualification and emergency triage by your rules, booking on your real calendar, SMS follow-up, call recordings and transcripts, a monthly tuning session</li>
          <li><strong>Not charged:</strong> no per-minute charges and no per-call charges. The Front Door website fee ($497) credits toward this build if started within 60 days.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The published price next to other ways of answering the phone is on
          the
          <a class="text-link" href="/learn/ai-receptionist-cost/">AI receptionist cost page</a>,
          and the service detail is on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/services/ai-front-desk">AI front desk page</a>.
          If your volume is low, your callers are English-only, and you only
          need a human to take messages, say so on the assessment. We will
          tell you if you should not buy this.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Call to action:</strong> Book the free 15-minute assessment at
          <a class="text-link" href="https://book.arkansasautomated.com/book/discovery-call">book.arkansasautomated.com</a>
          or use the <a class="text-link" href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
          Email <a class="text-link" href="mailto:dre@arkansasautomated.com">dre@arkansasautomated.com</a>
          if you would rather write. You hear it answer as your business before you pay anything.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Bilingual coverage statewide, not just NWA</h2>
        <p>
          Coverage is Fayetteville, Bentonville, Rogers, Springdale, the I-49
